Create a file named test_*.py, write functions prefixed with test_, and use assert statements. Example: def add(a, b): return a + b

def test_add_positive_numbers():
    assert add(2, 3) == 5

def test_add_negative_numbers():
    assert add(-1, -1) == -2. Run tests with the pytest command in your terminal. For more complex cases, use fixtures with @pytest.fixture to set up reusable test data, and pytest.raises() to test that specific exceptions are thrown.

Create a file ending in .test.js, and use describe and it (or test) blocks with expect assertions. Example: function add(a, b) { return a + b; }

test('adds 2 + 3 to equal 5', () => {
  expect(add(2, 3)).toBe(5);
});. For React components, use React Testing Library alongside Jest to render components and query the DOM the way a user would, using getByText, getByRole, etc., rather than testing internal implementation details.

Define an interface or type for the props and use it as the function parameter type. Example: interface ButtonProps {
  label: string;
  onClick: () => void;
  disabled?: boolean;
}

function Button({ label, onClick, disabled = false }: ButtonProps) {
  return <button onClick={onClick} disabled={disabled}>{label}</button>;
}. The question mark makes a prop optional. This catches errors at compile time if a required prop is missing or the wrong type is passed.

Delay the API call until the user stops typing for a set period, using a timer that resets on every keystroke. Example with a custom hook: function useDebounce(value, delay) {
  const [debounced, setDebounced] = useState(value);
  useEffect(() => {
    const timer = setTimeout(() => setDebounced(value), delay);
    return () => clearTimeout(timer);
  }, [value, delay]);
  return debounced;
}. Then use the debounced value as the dependency for your fetch call, so the API is only hit after the user pauses typing, typically 300-500ms.

Use React Navigation, the standard library for React Native routing. Set up a stack navigator for screen-to-screen flows like Home -> Details, and a tab navigator for top-level sections like Home, Search, Profile. Example: const Stack = createNativeStackNavigator();
function App() {
  return (
    <NavigationContainer>
      <Stack.Navigator>
        <Stack.Screen name="Home" component={HomeScreen} />
        <Stack.Screen name="Details" component={DetailsScreen} />
      </Stack.Navigator>
    </NavigationContainer>
  );
}. Nest navigators (tabs inside stacks, or vice versa) to build more complex navigation hierarchies.
